# 09-CRO / testing and experiments

**Goes here.** Whether a difference is real. Sample size and power, stopping rules,
what to do below the traffic threshold, prioritisation frameworks, test design, common
invalidations, session replay and qualitative research method, and the repair versus
test distinction.

## The anchor source

**CRO as testing and experimentation for DTC ecommerce** is the research document for
this folder. It covers sample size tables, the peeking problem, sequential methods,
Bayesian versus frequentist, CUPED, bandits, prioritisation, Shopify checkout limits,
and a stage-gated guide by monthly order volume. It carries a 38-source list with
tiers. Tag it `rights: owned`. Most other sources here should be checked against it.

## The test for whether a source belongs here

**Does it mention sample size, significance, or a stopping rule?**

If yes, it is CRO. If it is a list of page changes, it is 07-LPG however loudly the
title says "CRO". Most YouTube CRO content is page tactics with no statistics in it.

## Numbers every card here should respect

At a 2% baseline conversion rate, 95% confidence, 80% power:

```
detect a 20% lift    ~21,000 visitors per variant    ~260 conversions
detect a 10% lift    ~80,000 visitors per variant    ~1,000 conversions
detect a  5% lift    ~315,000 visitors per variant   ~3,800 conversions
```

A source that claims a meaningful test result below these volumes is either reporting
a very large effect or reporting noise. Usually noise.

