## Topics

sample size and power, stopping rules and peeking, sequential testing, low traffic
methods, bayesian versus frequentist, variance reduction, bandits, prioritisation
frameworks, test design and metrics, common invalidations, repair versus test,
research methods that generate hypotheses.

## Skip

Case studies claiming a lift with no sample size, duration and baseline. That is the
single most common source of library poison, and this field is full of it. Apply
Twyman's law: any figure that looks surprising is usually wrong.

## Scope mix

Mostly growth, because testing is gated on volume. The one baseline: broken things
get repaired, never tested.

